<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hōnen</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1133–<a href="/years/1212">1212</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Honen</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hōnen was a Japanese Buddhist monk who founded the Jōdo-shū, the Pure Land school, in 1175. He taught that salvation came through calling on the name of the Buddha <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amida">Amida</a> (the nembutsu) rather than through difficult practices, opening <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> to ordinary people. Opposed by the older schools, he was exiled in 1207, and his disciple <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shinran">Shinran</a> founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jodo-shinshu">True Pure Land school</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/honen.jpg" alt="Hōnen" width="320" height="390" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fujiwara, Takanobu (1142-1205) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Takanobu-no-miei.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Hōnen was born in Mimasaka Province, in what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/okayama">Okayama</a>, to a prominent family. His father, Uruma no Tokikuni, was a provincial official who headed the local police. In 1141 his father was assassinated by an official sent to govern the province, and is believed to have told his son not to hate the enemy but to become a monk.</p><p>Hōnen trained as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tendai">Tendai</a> monk and left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-hiei">Mount Hiei</a> in 1150 for the quieter temple of Kurodani. He later studied at Saga and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nara">Nara</a>, then returned to the libraries of Mount Hiei. He was struck by the contrast between the suffering of ordinary people and the lives of elite monks in ornate temples. At forty-three he read the Commentary on the Meditation Sutra by the Chinese Pure Land master Shandao.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Hōnen moved to the Ōtani district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a>, where he taught the recitation of the nembutsu to crowds of laymen and laywomen. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genpei-war">Genpei War</a> of 1180–1185 brought many people to the capital seeking refuge, and his message of universal salvation appealed to them. By 1204 he had around 190 disciples, a number taken from the signatures on the Seven Article Pledge.</p><p>Monks of other schools kept criticising him. In 1207 a ban on exclusive nembutsu was imposed after two of his followers held a retreat attended by court ladies, and the disciples were accused of using it as a cover for sexual liaisons. Hōnen, then eighty, was exiled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shikoku">Shikoku</a> together with disciples including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shinran">Shinran</a>, and four disciples were executed. He was later pardoned and returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a>, where he died in 1212.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Hōnen wrote his main work, the Senchakushū, in 1198 at the request of his patron Kujō Kanezane. In 1211 his disciples completed woodblocks for it, making it the first printed text of a Japanese Buddhist author. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tendai">Tendai</a> monks attacked the print shop and destroyed the blocks in 1227, and his followers cut them again in 1239.</p><p>He taught that rebirth in the Pure Land comes through reliance on the other-power of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amida">Amida</a>, expressed by reciting his name, and not through the self-power of difficult practice. He held that women, the uneducated and even those who had done the worst deeds could be born there, and that sincere faith mattered more than the number of recitations.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hōnen” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Tanjō-ji Temple, in what is now <a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a>. Died in Chion-in Temple, in what is now <a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=34.96,133.95&amp;year=1184" aria-label="Tanjō-ji Temple on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Hōnen"><image href="/maps/globes/g120n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5968 4518h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5968 4518h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=34.96,133.95&amp;year=1184">What was here in 1184? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
